      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles weak credential hashing / hardcoded-secret: a value named 'key'/'secret' is run through hash_secret() (a bare unsalted hashlib.sha256, no key stretching) and stored, which crypto scanners flag as CWE-916/327 like they would for password hashing. It is actually safe here: the hashed material 'raw' is a freshly generated high-entropy token from secrets.token_urlsafe(32) (CSPRNG, ~256 bits), not a low-entropy user password. For verifying high-entropy random API keys, a fast unsalted SHA-256 digest is the appropriate construction (salting/PBKDF2 are unnecessary because brute force is infeasible against 256-bit random tokens). Passwords in this app correctly use pbkdf2_sha256 in security.hash_password."
      },
      "false_positive_trap": true,
